# Mega Drive, SNES and PC Engine content. `.md` is the one entry in this list
# that is ALSO an extremely common non-ROM extension -- every Markdown file in
# every repository -- so it is deliberately anchored to the roms directories
# rather than applied globally: an unqualified `*.md` here would silently stop
# this project's own README.md, TESTED.md and docs/ from ever being committed
# again, which is a far worse failure than a stray ROM being offered.
# Checked before writing it this way: the author's 100k-file collection holds
# exactly one real Markdown file, and it is not in a Mega Drive directory.
roms/**/*.md
roms/*.md
*.sfc
*.smc
*.pce
# Game Boy Advance. Unambiguous -- .gba has never been anything else -- so it
# needs none of the qualification .md above does.
*.gba
# NOT *.sgx or *.chd: koboy does not list either (SuperGrafx needs a core this
# project does not ship, PC Engine CD needs a BIOS it must not ship), so
# neither can arrive here as content koboy put there.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- BIOS files
# Two systems need a console boot ROM koboy does not and must not ship
# (ColecoVision's colecovision.rom, Intellivision's exec.bin + grom.bin), and
# `tests/test_dist.sh` already asserts none of them reaches the package. This
# is the other half of that guard: it stops one reaching the REPOSITORY, which
# no test can check after the fact.
#
# `*.rom` and `*.bin` are global rather than anchored to the roms directories,
# unlike `.md` above, and the asymmetry is deliberate. `.md` had to be
# anchored because this project's own Markdown files end that way; nothing in
# this tree legitimately ends `.rom` or `.bin` -- every build output lives
# under build/ or dist/, both ignored above -- so the global form costs
# nothing and catches a BIOS dropped in the working directory during a test
# run, which is exactly how they arrive.
*.rom
*.bin

# ----------------------------------------------- save data the CORES write
# Not ROMs, but not ours either: these are files a core creates beside the
# content while a game runs, and five of them were committed by accident
# before this rule existed (two Pokemon Mini `.eep`, three FinalBurn Neo
# high-score files). `*.srm`, `*.sav`, `*.ngf` and `*.flash` are already
# covered above; these are the rest of the set, one per core that does its
# own persistence rather than going through sram.c.
*.eep
*.hi
*.fs
*.nv
# koboy's own save states: state_path writes "<rom>.srm.stN", N in 1..3.
*.st[0-9]
# FinalBurn Neo's save directory, which the core mkdir's itself beside the
# binary on every load. Anchored to the repository root so it cannot shadow
# third_party/fbneo/ (already ignored above) or a future src/fbneo/.
/fbneo/
